The following journal articles provide a good foundation of research using the OSNAP data at Greenland's southern tip. 

Le Bras, I. A.-A., Straneo, F., Holte, J., & Holliday, N. P. (2018). Seasonality of Freshwater in the East Greenland Current System From 2014 to 2016. Journal of Geophysical Research: Oceans, 123 (12), 8828–8848. https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/2018JC014511. doi:10.1029/2018JC014511

Le Bras, I. A.-A., Straneo, F., Holte, J.,de Jong, M. F., & Holliday, N. P.(2020). Rapid export of waters formedby convection near the Irminger Sea's western boundary. Geophysical Research Letters, 47, e2019GL085989. https://doi.org/10.1029/2019GL085989 

Lin, P., Pickart, R. S., Torres, D. J., & Pacini, A. (2018). Evolution of the Freshwater Coastal Current at the Southern Tip of Greenland. Journal of Physical Oceanography, 48 (9), 2127–2140. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/48/9/jpo-d-18-0035.1.xml. doi: 10.1175/JPO-D-18-0035.1

Pacini, A., Pickart, R. S., Bahr, F., Torres, D. J., Ramsey, A. L., Holte, J., Jong, M. F. d. (2020). Mean Conditions and Seasonality of the West Greenland Boundary Current System near Cape Farewell. Journal of Physical Oceanography, 50 (10), 2849–2871. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/50/10/jpoD200086.xml. doi: 10.1175/JPO-D-20-0086.1

Pacini, A., Pickart, R. S., Bras, I. A. L., Straneo, F., Holliday, N. P., & Spall, M. A. (2021). Cyclonic Eddies in the West Greenland Boundary Current System. Journal of Physical Oceanography, 51 (7), 2087–2102. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/51/7/JPO-D-20-0255.1.xml. doi: 10.1175/JPO-D-20-0255.1

Foukal, N. P., & Pickart, R. S. (2023). Moored Observations of the West Greenland Coastal Current along the Southwest Greenland Shelf. Journal of Physical Oceanography, 53 (11), 2619–2632. Retrieved 2024-04-11, from https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/53/11/JPO-D-23-0104.1.xml doi: 10.1175/JPO-D-23-0104.1

Some handy tips:
The "CF" moorings are the array just east of Greenland (CF stands for Cape Farewell). 
From 2014-2024, the CF array consisted of 7 moorings (CF1-CF7). In 2024, only 6 moorings were redeployed (CF1-CF3, CF5-CF7) 

The "LS" moorings are the array just west of Greenland (LS stands for Labrador Sea).
This array has changed much more than the CF array over time. Initially 7 moorings were deployed (LS1-LS7), but subsequently more moorings were added to extend the line (LSA, LSB, LSI, LS8).
In 2024 only 6 moorings were redeployed (LSA, LS1, LS3, LS5-LS7).
